      "decoded_text": "The main tradeoff between latency and throughput in online serving is that reducing latency generally increases throughput, and vice versa. This is because latency is the time it takes for a request to be served, while throughput is the number of requests that can be served per unit of time. To reduce latency, the system must process more requests per unit of time, thus increasing throughput. Conversely, to increase throughput, the system must process more requests per unit of time, which may increase latency. Therefore, the balance between latency and throughput is a key consideration in designing online serving systems, and it ultimately depends on the specific requirements of the application."
